  "stdout": "{\n  \"draft_file_sha256\": \"df55fba908a3ecb84bc70d13bb59241960ce1043418769cd6b3bc1035e69b4a2\",\n  \"schema\": \"s10y-choice-draft-v1\",\n  \"seed_sha256\": \"e384eb63ef8b0ec1aa3ed4f579abd983539abccdc98432de352d340909ed93fd\",\n  \"new_figures\": [],\n  \"coverage\": [\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"102\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q01\",\n        \"q02\",\n        \"q03\",\n        \"q04\",\n        \"q05\",\n        \"q06\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing the substitution-elimination steps for all six systems is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a through f map respectively to q01 through q06. All complete ordered results are represented; the coefficient systems are mathematically independent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"103\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q07\",\n        \"q08\",\n        \"q09\",\n        \"q10\",\n        \"q11\",\n        \"q12\",\n        \"q13\",\n        \"q14\",\n        \"q15\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently producing substitution, expansion/factorization and complete written solves for all nine systems is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a through i map respectively to q07 through q15. Each distinct polynomial/factor structure is an independent task. Full sets retain both roots where present and unique solutions in c and g.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"104\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q16\",\n        \"q17\",\n        \"q18\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Part a: drawing the circle and line and solving by reading their plotted intersections.\",\n        \"Part b: drawing the circle and hyperbola and locating all four intersections from the drawing.\",\n        \"Part c: drawing the circle and parabola, detecting their bottom tangency and locating every intersection graphically.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a,b,c map to q16,q17,q18. All exact intersections are represented, including the zero-root tangent contact in c. These are analytic configuration-recognition tasks, not graph execution or evidence of producing the source drawings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"105\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q19\",\n        \"q20\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing the two three-equation inconsistency arguments is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a and b map to q19 and q20. Both empty intersections are explicitly represented; checking only two equations is contrasted with satisfying all three.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"106\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q21\",\n        \"q22\",\n        \"q23\",\n        \"q24\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Writing the original denominator exclusions, elimination and verification for all four systems is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a through d map to q21 through q24. Every full set, both negative solutions in b and d, directed reciprocal differences and x≠0,y≠0 domains remain explicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"107\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q25\",\n        \"q26\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently formulating and solving both number-problem systems is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Part a is the unordered sum/product pair in q25; part b is the full signed difference/product set in q26. Distinct data and directed versus symmetric conditions justify separate whole-response units.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"108\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q27\",\n        \"q28\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently producing the positive-side geometric models and written derivations is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Part a's rectangle sides are q27; part b's right-triangle area is q28. Different geometric objects and requested quantities justify separate units, preserving perimeter, diagonal/hypotenuse, positivity and area roles.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"109\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q29\",\n        \"q30\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently modeling area/perimeter changes and solving with feasible assigned side roles is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Part a's assigned reductions and infeasible swapped geometry are q29. Part b's two valid original side assignments with +8/+2 roles are q30. Neither is silently reduced to one unqualified numeric root.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"110\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q31\",\n        \"q32\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing the digit/place-value models and excluding invalid auxiliary roots is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Part a is q31, including the actual digit-sum check that rejects N=28. Part b is q32, explicitly interpreting the same two digits after +27 as reversal rather than equal digit sum.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"111\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q33\",\n        \"q34\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing both travel systems, converting minute intervals and rejecting negative speeds is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Part a's A/B riders and later arrival are q33; part b's M/N trains and earlier arrival are q34. All source distances, meeting intervals, full-route arrival differences, station roles and units remain visible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"112\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q35\",\n        \"q36\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently producing the rate equations and positive full-job time solutions is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Part a's simultaneous full-tank versus sequential half-tank work is q35; part b's fast/slow individual harvest times is q36. Separate physical rate models justify whole-result units.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"113\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q37\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing the requested general proof for arbitrary a,b is deferred; recognizing a supplied identity is not proof production.\",\n        \"Independently writing the two named substitutions is not demonstrated.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"q37 accounts for named parts a and b and the general coordinate-swap proof claim. The correct alternative supplies an arbitrary-variable identity, not merely two confirming examples. No claim of proving the source writing capacity is made.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"114\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q38\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing the exact radical substitutions for the two original pairs is deferred.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Both original parts a and b are literal candidate pairs in one native multiple task for the same equation. Their products and exact totals are retained internally; additional candidates test omission or sign change of xy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"115\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q39\",\n        \"q40\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Part a: independently generating any two solutions of sqrt(x)-sqrt(y)=1.\",\n        \"Part b: independently generating any two solutions of sqrt(x+y)-sqrt(y)=1.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a and b map to q39 and q40. Each recognizes two distinct complete solution pairs and tests its real radicand domains. Supplying candidates changes generation to recognition; that original production capacity remains deferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"source_number\": \"116\",\n      \"question_ids\": [\n        \"q41\"\n      ],\n      \"omitted_tasks\": [\n        \"Independently writing the sign analysis leading to all four quadrant classifications is not demonstrated.\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Parts a through d are all rows in q41's coherent comparison table. All requested quadrant distinctions and the separate axis points are represented, including the negative portions of 9-x^2; no graph is requested or claimed.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n{\"id\":\"q01\",\"source_numbers\":[\"102\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the full linear system by substitution, retaining coordinate order.\",\"source_solution\":\"5(3y+5)+11y=-1 gives 26y=-26, y=-1, x=2.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One ordered pair settles this independent system; x and y are not separate response units.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16,25],\"limitations\":[\"Selection does not demonstrate independently producing substitution steps.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace 26y=-26 by 26y=26; y=1 and x=3+5=8.\",\"misconception\":\"Drop the negative sign on the isolated right-hand side.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"y=-1 gives x=2 and 10-11=-1.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve x=2,y=-1, then report the coordinates as (y,x).\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the order of an ordered solution pair.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q02\",\"source_numbers\":[\"102\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the system with a negative fractional coordinate.\",\"source_solution\":\"7(-2y-1)-20y=146 gives -34y=153; y=-9/2 and x=8.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One full pair preserves the coupled values and their order.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16,25],\"limitations\":[\"Written substitution production is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"x=8,y=-9/2 gives 56+90=146 and -2y-1=8.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Report the solved pair (8,-9/2) in reversed coordinate order.\",\"misconception\":\"Exchange x and y in the final pair.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Divide 153 by +34 instead of -34, obtaining y=9/2, then x=-2(9/2)-1=-10.\",\"misconception\":\"Lose the sign of the coefficient during division.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q03\",\"source_numbers\":[\"102\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Isolate y and substitute to determine the whole solution.\",\"source_solution\":\"y=12x-16 leads to 41x-48=75; x=3, y=20.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The independent system yields one pair, not two coordinate questions.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16,25],\"limitations\":[\"Selection does not establish written elimination competence.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Obtain x=3,y=20 but output (20,3).\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse coordinate roles.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Misread 41x-48=75 as 41x=-75-48=-123; x=-3, y=12(-3)-16=-52.\",\"misconception\":\"Negate both constants instead of adding 48 to both sides.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"41x=123 gives x=3; y=36-16=20; 15+60=75.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q04\",\"source_numbers\":[\"102\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve both equations simultaneously using an isolated expression.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=(2-6y)/7 leads to 12-71y=651, y=-9, x=8.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One full pair represents this independent system.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16,25],\"limitations\":[\"Writing the substitution and elimination is not assessed.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"56-54=2 and 48+45=93.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use +71y=639 instead of -71y=639; y=9 and x=(2-54)/7=-52/7.\",\"misconception\":\"Discard the negative coefficient after substitution.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Reverse the independently obtained coordinates x=8,y=-9.\",\"misconception\":\"Confuse ordered-pair coordinate positions.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q05\",\"source_numbers\":[\"102\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Find the simultaneous solution with the given unequal coefficients.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=(43-3y)/4 leads to 215-43y=172, y=1, x=10.\",\"unit_reason\":\"This coefficient system is independent; its complete result is one pair.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16,25],\"limitations\":[\"Written substitution steps remain unassessed.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve (10,1), then write (1,10).\",\"misconception\":\"Exchange coordinate order.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"40+3=43 and 50-7=43.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Change -43y=-43 into 43y=-43; y=-1, x=(43+3)/4=23/2.\",\"misconception\":\"Negate only one side when removing a minus sign.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q06\",\"source_numbers\":[\"102\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the system after isolating x.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=(4+9y)/8 leads to -12+5y=8, y=4, x=5.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Use a whole pair rather than separate arithmetic clicks.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16,25],\"limitations\":[\"Independent written elimination is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Change 5y=20 to 5y=-20, then y=-4 and x=(4-36)/8=-4.\",\"misconception\":\"Introduce an unjustified negative sign when isolating y.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Output (y,x) after obtaining x=5,y=4.\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse ordered-pair positions.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"40-36=4 and -15+16=1.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q07\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Find both quadratic branches and their matching x values.\",\"source_solution\":\"Substitution yields (y-1)(y-3)=0; matching x=y+2 gives (3,1),(5,3).\",\"unit_reason\":\"The complete solution set, including both branches, is a single response unit.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Does not assess writing the substitution or factorization.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find y=1,3 and x=3,5 but cross-pair x=3 with y=3 and x=5 with y=1.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat the two root lists as independently interchangeable.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"y=1 gives x=3; y=3 gives x=5; 9-2=7 and 25-18=7.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Keep roots y=1,3 but use x=y instead of x=y+2 in back-substitution.\",\"misconception\":\"Drop the constant offset when reconstructing x.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q08\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the quadratic after substitution and retain the negative branch.\",\"source_solution\":\"3y^2+8y-3=(3y-1)(y+3)=0, hence (3,1/3),(-7,-3).\",\"unit_reason\":\"Both solutions and their correspondence form one complete set.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Written algebraic production is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"For y=1/3,-3, x=3,-7 respectively; 9-2=7 and 49-42=7.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use the correct y roots but x=3y-2: x=-1,-11.\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the sign of the constant only in back-substitution.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Cross the two matched y values between the computed x values.\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the dependence x=3y+2 when pairing roots.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q09\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Use the shared factor x-5y rather than assume every quadratic system has two solutions.\",\"source_solution\":\"x(x-5y)=10 and x-5y=1 imply x=10,y=9/5.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The factor identity determines one entire solution set.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Writing the factorization is not demonstrated.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Obtain x=10, then replace x-5y=1 with x+5y=1 to get y=-9/5.\",\"misconception\":\"Change the sign of the y term during back-substitution.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Reverse the coordinates of (10,9/5).\",\"misconception\":\"Exchange x and y in the final ordered pair.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"10-9=1 and 100-90=10; the first factor identity forces x=10 uniquely.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q10\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Retain both roots with the correct linear back-substitution.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=(y+3)/2 gives y^2+5y-6=0; y=1,-6 and x=2,-3/2.\",\"unit_reason\":\"A complete set preserves the fractional negative branch without fragmenting coordinates.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Substitution and quadratic solving production are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"10-1=9 and 45-36=9; each pair also has 2x-y=3.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Cross-pair the two x values with the opposite y roots.\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the correspondence imposed by 2x-y=3.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"After finding y=1,-6, use x=(y-3)/2, giving -1,-9/2.\",\"misconception\":\"Transpose 3 with the wrong sign in reconstructing x.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q11\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Determine the two intersections, including the rational negative solution.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=2(y+1)/3 gives 13y^2+8y-896=0 with discriminant 216^2; y=8,-112/13 yields x=6,-66/13.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Both intersections are one complete solution-set result.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2,16],\"limitations\":[\"Independent quadratic derivation is not assessed.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Cross-pair the computed x values 6,-66/13 with the opposite y roots.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat coordinate lists as independent rather than matched solutions.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace y=-112/13 by its absolute value 112/13; x=2(112/13+1)/3=250/39.\",\"misconception\":\"Assume squared equations require positive coordinates.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"The quadratic has exactly these two roots; 36+64=100 and (4356+12544)/169=100, with 3x-2y=2 for both.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q12\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Preserve both signs while enforcing the linear ratio.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=3y/2 gives (3/2)y^2=24, y=4,-4; corresponding x=6,-6.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The complete paired sign branches form one mathematical result.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Does not demonstrate writing a quadratic solution.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find absolute magnitudes 6,4 but independently choose opposite coordinate signs.\",\"misconception\":\"Forget that 2x=3y requires the same sign.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Each pair has 2x=3y and 72-48=24; y^2=16 supplies exactly two branches.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Keep y=4,-4 but use the reciprocal ratio x=2y/3 instead of 3y/2.\",\"misconception\":\"Invert the coefficient ratio while isolating x.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q13\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Exploit the difference of squares and the fixed nonzero factor.\",\"source_solution\":\"(2x-3y)(2x+3y)=15 gives 2x-3y=3; combined with 2x+3y=5 gives x=2,y=1/3.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One factor-based solution set is a complete response.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Production of the factorization and resulting linear solve is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace 2x-3y=15/5=3 by -3; together with 2x+3y=5 this gives x=1/2,y=4/3.\",\"misconception\":\"Insert a minus sign into the quotient of the factors.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Report (y,x) after solving (x,y)=(2,1/3).\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the ordered pair.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"4x=8 and 6y=2; 16-1=15 and 4+1=5.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q14\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the mixed quadratic with the correct affine correspondence.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=2y+1 gives 5y^2+5y-10=0, y=1,-2; x=3,-3.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Both solutions of this independent quadratic are one complete set.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Writing the expansion and factorization is not assessed.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"9+3-1=11 and 9+6-4=11; both satisfy x-2y=1.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use the correct y roots but reconstruct x=2y-1, producing 1,-5.\",\"misconception\":\"Transpose the constant with the wrong sign.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Cross-pair x=3,-3 with y=-2,1 rather than the corresponding roots.\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the affine relation while forming the set.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q15\",\"source_numbers\":[\"103\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Find both x roots and reconstruct their negative y coordinates.\",\"source_solution\":\"y=(-1-3x)/2 gives x^2-8x+15=0, x=3,5; y=-5,-8.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The solution set keeps both branches and coordinate correspondence together.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,16],\"limitations\":[\"Written elimination and root finding are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Cross-pair x=3,5 with the opposite computed y values.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat reconstructed coordinates as interchangeable.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"9-15+15=9 and 25-40+24=9; 9-10=-1 and 15-16=-1.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Keep roots x=3,5 but use y=(1-3x)/2, obtaining -4,-7.\",\"misconception\":\"Change the constant -1 to +1 during back-substitution.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q16\",\"source_numbers\":[\"104\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Recognize all common points of the circle and line.\",\"source_solution\":\"y=x-5 gives 2x(x-5)=0; intersections (0,-5),(5,0). The line is a secant.\",\"unit_reason\":\"All intersections constitute one whole graphical relationship, not point-by-point clicks.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"No evidence of drawing both curves, reading coordinates from a plotted graph, or executing the source graphical method.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve x-y=5 for the two axis intercepts but replace y=-5 at x=0 by +5.\",\"misconception\":\"Use unsigned intercept distances as signed coordinates.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace x-y=5 with x-y=-5; y=x+5 gives the circle intersections (-5,0),(0,5).\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the sign of the entire line's constant.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"x=0,5 yields y=-5,0; both have squared radius 25 and difference 5.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q17\",\"source_numbers\":[\"104\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Preserve all four circle-hyperbola intersections and the opposite-sign condition.\",\"source_solution\":\"(x+y)^2=4, (x-y)^2=36 give (4,-2),(-2,4),(2,-4),(-4,2).\",\"unit_reason\":\"One complete set captures sign branches and all intersections together.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2,26],\"limitations\":[\"Drawing the circle and hyperbola and extracting their intersections graphically remain deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"The four sign combinations of sums ±2 and differences ±6 give precisely these points; each product is -8 and squared radius 20.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace xy=-8 by xy=8 while keeping the circle; magnitudes 4,2 paired with matching signs give these four points.\",\"misconception\":\"Drop the negative product sign.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find squared magnitudes 16,4 but interpret xy<0 as requiring y<0 alone; allow either sign for x and always choose negative y.\",\"misconception\":\"Fail to link the two coordinate signs in a negative product.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q18\",\"source_numbers\":[\"104\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Recognize every intersection, including the bottom tangency rather than losing its zero root.\",\"source_solution\":\"For u=x^2>=0, u+(u/2-10)^2=100 gives u(u-36)=0. Thus (0,-10),(±6,8). At (0,-10) both tangents are horizontal y=-10 and the parabola remains above the lower circle nearby; the other intersections are transverse.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The full intersection configuration with its tangency is one coherent response; a count alone would omit the key geometry.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2,26],\"limitations\":[\"Recognizing the analytic configuration is not producing or inspecting a drawn graph.\",\"Accurate plotting and graphical location of all intersections and the tangent contact are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Retain correct abscissas 0,±6 but reconstruct y using x^2-10 instead of x^2/2-10, giving -10,26,26.\",\"misconception\":\"Omit the coefficient 1/2 only when converting abscissas to ordinates.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"u=0,36 yields all three points. Near zero the circle lower branch is -10+x^2/20+..., versus -10+x^2/2; at ±6 the circle and parabola have distinct slopes, so only the bottom contact is tangent.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find the three common points and classify every equality point as a crossing without checking local side changes; this misclassifies the double contact at x=0.\",\"misconception\":\"Equate being a common point with crossing through, ignoring tangency.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q19\",\"source_numbers\":[\"105\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Intersect all three equations rather than accept a pair satisfying only two.\",\"source_solution\":\"First two force (5,-5), for which x^2-y^2=0≠40; the full system has no solution.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The whole three-equation intersection, including inconsistency, is the response.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"Writing the consistency argument independently is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve x+y=0 and x-y=10 only, obtaining x=5,y=-5.\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the third equation after solving the first two.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use x-y=10 and (x-y)(x+y)=40 to get x+y=4, hence (7,-3).\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the required first equation x+y=0.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"The unique pair from the first two fails the third, so their three-way intersection is empty.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q20\",\"source_numbers\":[\"105\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Enforce simultaneous truth of three equations.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=1+2y substituted into 3x-4y=3 forces y=0,x=1, which has x^2+y^2=1, not 2.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One whole intersection tests inconsistency rather than separate equation checks.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"Written proof of inconsistency is not demonstrated.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"The only common point of the two lines, (1,0), is not on the circle of squared radius 2.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve only the two linear equations, obtaining (1,0), and stop.\",\"misconception\":\"Assume satisfying two equations settles a three-equation system.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use x=1+2y in x^2+y^2=2, giving 5y^2+4y-1=0, y=1/5,-1 and x=7/5,-1.\",\"misconception\":\"Drop 3x-4y=3 while intersecting the other two equations.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q21\",\"source_numbers\":[\"106\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the reciprocal system within its nonzero domain and retain both coordinate assignments.\",\"source_solution\":\"Multiplying by xy≠0 gives x+y=(5/12)xy, so xy=24. The roots of t^2-10t+24=0 are 4,6, giving (4,6),(6,4).\",\"unit_reason\":\"One full set preserves the sum, product, domain and both assignments.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"Selection does not demonstrate writing the domain restriction and reciprocal elimination.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find scalar roots 4 and 6 but put each root in both coordinate positions, producing (4,4),(6,6).\",\"misconception\":\"Assume both variables take the same root instead of enforcing their sum.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Both nonzero pairs have sum 10 and product 24; (x+y)/(xy)=10/24=5/12.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Negate the roots x=4,6 to -4,-6, then enforce y=10-x to obtain 14,16.\",\"misconception\":\"Negate the roots of the quadratic without changing its coefficients.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q22\",\"source_numbers\":[\"106\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Retain the negative solution of the reciprocal difference system.\",\"source_solution\":\"(1/y-1/x)=(x-y)/(xy) gives xy=40. With x=y+6, y^2+6y-40=0 gives y=4,-10 and x=10,-4.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The full solution set keeps the negative branch and the directed difference together.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"Independent writing of denominator restrictions, elimination and negative-root verification is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve (10,4),(-4,-10) and reverse the coordinates of each pair.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat a directed difference as unchanged by coordinate reversal.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Keep x=10 but replace the second x root -4 by |−4|=4; reconstruct y=x-6 to get -2.\",\"misconception\":\"Discard a negative x root by taking its absolute value instead of testing it.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Both pairs have difference 6 and nonzero product 40, hence reciprocal difference 6/40=3/20.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q23\",\"source_numbers\":[\"106\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Solve the decimal reciprocal system with exact fractions and matching coordinates.\",\"source_solution\":\"1.2=6/5 and y=5-2x give 12x^2-35x+25=0. Roots x=5/3,5/4 correspond to y=5/3,5/2; all denominators are nonzero.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Both paired solutions are one complete result rather than independent coordinate questions.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"Written reciprocal transformation and quadratic derivation remain deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"The reciprocal sums are 3/5+3/5 and 4/5+2/5; both equal 6/5, and 2x+y=5.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find the two x roots and two y values correctly, then cross the correspondence.\",\"misconception\":\"Pair roots independently instead of obeying y=5-2x.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Keep x=5/3,5/4 but reconstruct y=5-x rather than 5-2x, giving 10/3,15/4.\",\"misconception\":\"Omit the coefficient 2 during back-substitution.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q24\",\"source_numbers\":[\"106\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Preserve both real branches, including negative coordinates, with a nonzero domain.\",\"source_solution\":\"y=2x+3, (y-x)/(xy)=1/10 lead to 2x^2-7x-30=0. x=6,-5/2 gives y=15,-2; both pairs are nonzero.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One complete set represents both branches and the directed reciprocal relation.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1,2],\"limitations\":[\"Does not demonstrate writing the denominator exclusions or checking the negative branch independently.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find x=6,-5/2, then use y=2x-3 rather than 2x+3 to obtain 9,-8.\",\"misconception\":\"Transpose 3 with the wrong sign.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"1/6-1/15=1/10 and -2/5-(-1/2)=1/10; both have y-2x=3.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace the second root -5/2 by its absolute value 5/2, then calculate y=2(5/2)+3=8.\",\"misconception\":\"Assume real reciprocal solutions must have positive x.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q25\",\"source_numbers\":[\"107\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Determine an unordered number pair from both sum and product.\",\"source_solution\":\"The two numbers are the roots of t^2-20t+96=(t-8)(t-12)=0.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Both numbers jointly determine the answer; their order is irrelevant.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independently modeling and solving the quadratic is not demonstrated.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Split the sum equally, 20/2=10, and assign both numbers 10.\",\"misconception\":\"Use the sum alone and assume equal numbers, neglecting the product 96.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use t^2+20t+96=(t+8)(t+12) instead of t^2-20t+96.\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the sign connecting the quadratic coefficient to the required sum.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"8+12=20 and 8·12=96; the quadratic fixes this unordered pair.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q26\",\"source_numbers\":[\"107\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Find both signed number pairs with a directed difference.\",\"source_solution\":\"x=y+6 gives y^2+6y-216=(y-12)(y+18)=0, yielding (18,12),(-12,-18).\",\"unit_reason\":\"The two signed possibilities form one complete set; restricting to positive numbers would lose a source solution.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Written modeling and solving both branches are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Both products are 216 and differences x-y are 6; -12 is greater than -18.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Reverse each correct ordered pair without rechecking the directed difference.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat x-y=6 as symmetric in x and y.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find the positive pair (18,12), then negate both coordinates in place to create a second pair.\",\"misconception\":\"Assume simultaneous negation preserves a nonzero difference; it actually changes 6 to -6.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q27\",\"source_numbers\":[\"108\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Model perimeter and diagonal using positive side lengths.\",\"source_solution\":\"a+b=41 and a^2+b^2=841 give ab=420, so a,b are 20,21 m.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The physical pair of side lengths is one whole geometric result.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Writing the perimeter/Pythagorean model and solving it is not demonstrated.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Obtain side lengths 20,21, then double each to 40,42 because there are two edges of each length.\",\"misconception\":\"Report each pair's perimeter contribution instead of individual side length.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"2(20+21)=82 and sqrt(400+441)=29; both lengths are positive.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Derive ab=420 but assume a=b, giving both lengths sqrt(420).\",\"misconception\":\"Impose a square without checking the distinct perimeter and diagonal constraints.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q28\",\"source_numbers\":[\"108\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Use positive legs, perimeter and hypotenuse to obtain triangle area.\",\"source_solution\":\"Legs have sum 47 and squared sum 1369, so product (47^2-37^2)/2=420; area is 210 cm^2.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The requested area is a whole result of the geometric model, not separate clicks for each leg.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent production of the geometric equations and area derivation is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find the leg product ab=420 and report it directly as area.\",\"misconception\":\"Use the rectangle product rather than half the product for a triangle.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find ab=420, correctly halve to 210, then halve a second time to 105.\",\"misconception\":\"Apply the triangle factor 1/2 twice.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"ab=(2209-1369)/2=420; area=ab/2=210 cm^2.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q29\",\"source_numbers\":[\"109\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Model area/perimeter changes and retain feasible assigned side reductions.\",\"source_solution\":\"New sides sum to 46 and multiply to 240, so their values are 40,6. Only L=40,W=6 decreases both original sides; reductions are 20,2 m.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The assigned length/width reduction pair is one meaningful physical result; swapping them changes feasibility.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent modeling, solving, and rejection of physically impossible assignments are not demonstrated.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"New dimensions 40×6 give area 240 and perimeter 92; original perimeter 136 falls by 44.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find reductions 20,2 but assign 2 to length and 20 to width.\",\"misconception\":\"Exchange side roles without checking that the new width 8-20 is negative.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Halve each original side separately: reductions 60/2=30 and 8/2=4.\",\"misconception\":\"Interpret halving area as halving both side lengths; that quarters area and changes the perimeter decrease.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q30\",\"source_numbers\":[\"109\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Find every positive original dimension assignment while preserving which side receives each increment.\",\"source_solution\":\"a+b=40, (a+8)(b+2)=3ab/2 give a^2-52a+672=0, hence (24,16),(28,12). Both are valid; new dimensions are (32,18),(36,14).\",\"unit_reason\":\"All possible assigned dimensions are one coherent result; neither printed side labels nor a desire for uniqueness justify dropping a branch.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent formulation and solving of the incremental-area system are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve using a+8,b+2, then swap coordinates in each original dimension pair.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat dimension order as immaterial even though a and b have different increments.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"24+16=28+12=40; 32·18=576=1.5·384 and 36·14=504=1.5·336.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find original dimensions (24,16),(28,12), then add (8,2) and report the resulting dimensions instead.\",\"misconception\":\"Confuse final dimensions with the requested original dimensions.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q31\",\"source_numbers\":[\"110\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Enforce the actual decimal digit sum, not merely a free auxiliary variable.\",\"source_solution\":\"For number N and digit sum s, N+2s=96,Ns=952 imply N^2-96N+1904=0 with roots 68,28. Only 68 has the required actual sum 14; 28 would require s=34 although its digits sum to 10.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The original two-digit number is the whole task; constraints on its digits eliminate the extraneous modeled root.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independently creating the digit model and checking its auxiliary variable is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Solve N^2-96N+1904=0 and retain root N=28 with s=(96-28)/2=34.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat s as independent instead of verifying that it equals 2+8.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find tens digit 6 and units digit 8 but assemble the number as 10·8+6=86.\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse place-value roles when reporting the number.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"6+8=14; 68+2·14=96 and 68·14=952.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q32\",\"source_numbers\":[\"110\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Interpret the same two digits as reversal and preserve original versus reversed number roles.\",\"source_solution\":\"For tens u and units v, 10u+v+27=10v+u gives v-u=3. u^2+v^2=65 then gives u=4,v=7; the other root has negative digits.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The whole original number combines place value, reversal and square sum; no separate digit clicks.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent digit-equation modeling and invalid-root exclusion are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"4^2+7^2=65 and 47+27=74, its reversal.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find 47+27=74 but report the number after addition rather than the original.\",\"misconception\":\"Exchange original and reversed-number roles.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Recognize 65=1^2+8^2 and take digits 1,8 without applying the reversal equation.\",\"misconception\":\"Use only the square-sum decomposition; 18+27=45 does not reverse the digits.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q33\",\"source_numbers\":[\"111\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Combine meeting speed sum with directed full-route arrival difference in consistent units.\",\"source_solution\":\"u+v=28 and 28/u-28/v=7/12 give u=12 or 112; positivity rejects 112 with v=-84. Thus A rider 12,B rider 16 km/h.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The two role-assigned speeds constitute one whole travel result; their order is essential.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent travel modeling, minute-to-hour conversion and invalid speed rejection are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find speed magnitudes 12,16 but assign the faster speed to the rider stated to arrive later.\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the directed arrival-time relation.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"12+16=28; 28/12-28/16=7/12 h=35 min, with the A rider later.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Split the meeting speed sum equally, 28/2=14, for both riders.\",\"misconception\":\"Use the meeting condition only and neglect the unequal full-route arrival times.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q34\",\"source_numbers\":[\"111\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Keep station roles, meeting interval and directed full-route arrival times distinct.\",\"source_solution\":\"p+q=135 and 45/q-45/p=3/20 give q=60 or 675. Positivity rejects 675 with p=-540. M train 75,N train 60 km/h.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One assigned speed pair captures the entire travel problem and its units.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independently writing the speed/time system, unit conversion and invalid-root check is deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find magnitudes 75,60 but give 60 to the M train, reversing which train arrives first.\",\"misconception\":\"Reverse the station-specific arrival order.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"From 45/(20/60)=135 divide the combined speed equally: 135/2=67.5.\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the 9-minute arrival difference and assume equal speeds.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"(75+60)/3=45 km at meeting; 45/60-45/75=0.15 h=9 min.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q35\",\"source_numbers\":[\"112\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Distinguish individual full-tank times, sequential half-tank times and combined rates.\",\"source_solution\":\"Full-tank times t,s>0 obey 1/t+1/s=1/12 and (t+s)/2=25, giving t+s=50,ts=600 and times 20,30 min.\",\"unit_reason\":\"The unordered pair of full-job times is one coherent rate task.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent rate modeling and quadratic solving are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"1/20+1/30=1/12 and 20/2+30/2=25 min.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find full-tank times 20,30 but report their halves 10,15, which are the sequential half-tank durations.\",\"misconception\":\"Confuse half-job durations with the requested individual full-job times.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Assign the joint filling duration 12 min to each individual pipe.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat combined performance as each pipe's individual performance.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q36\",\"source_numbers\":[\"112\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Use reciprocal work rates, directed time difference and positive durations.\",\"source_solution\":\"Fast time t>0, slow t+24; 1/t+1/(t+24)=1/35 gives t^2-46t-840=0, t=60 or -14. Reject -14; times 60,84 h.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One role-assigned time pair is the whole work-rate answer.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Independent rate equation production and negative-time rejection are deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Find durations 60,84 but report 84 for the faster machine.\",\"misconception\":\"Associate greater completion time with faster work.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"84-60=24 and 1/60+1/84=1/35.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Give the faster machine the joint time 35, then add 24 for the slower machine to obtain 59.\",\"misconception\":\"Confuse joint full-job time with the fast machine's individual full-job time.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q37\",\"source_numbers\":[\"113\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Cover both named substitutions and recognize the general symmetry proof, not infer universality from two examples.\",\"source_solution\":\"F(5,1)=25+15+1=41 and F(1,5)=1+15+25=41. For all real a,b, F(b,a)=b^2+3ba+a^2=a^2+3ab+b^2=F(a,b), proving the implication.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Finite checks and the universal coordinate-swap claim form one coherent symmetry task; the option contains a mathematical argument, not a truth-combination code.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Recognizing an identity-based argument is not independently writing the requested general proof.\",\"The source general proof-writing production task remains deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Verify both named pairs, then require (b,a)=(a,b) as a condition for remaining a solution; equality of pairs forces a=b.\",\"misconception\":\"Confuse equality to the original pair with membership in the same equation's solution set; (5,1) is already a counterexample.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace the swapped product ba by b^2. For original a=5,b=1 the supposed swapped value is 1+3+25=29, leading to rejection of (1,5).\",\"misconception\":\"Replace a product of different coordinates by the square of one coordinate when swapping.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Both substitutions total 41, and commutativity of addition and multiplication gives the displayed identity for arbitrary a,b, not only these examples.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q38\",\"source_numbers\":[\"114\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Judge both original radical pairs by exact substitution, especially the mixed product.\",\"source_solution\":\"Original a has squared terms 12,3 and product 6, total 21, so is not a solution. Original b has sum 6 and product 7, giving (x+y)^2-xy=36-7=29, so is a solution.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Native selection of qualifying pairs preserves the shared equation without per-expression clicks or combination-code answers.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Selecting pairs does not demonstrate independently writing the two exact radical computations.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"This candidate is the mandatory original source pair, not a generated numeric distractor. A domain-only test checks 12>=0 and 3>=0 and accepts it without substitution; the actual substitution is 12+sqrt(36)+3=21, not 29.\",\"misconception\":\"Confuse real-domain admissibility with satisfying the equation: check only that the two supplied square roots exist, then accept the pair without evaluating the left side.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"xy=(3+sqrt(2))(3-sqrt(2))=7 and x+y=6, so the left side is 36-7=29.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Choose squared magnitudes 12 and 29-12=17 while neglecting xy; the actual total is 29+sqrt(204)>29.\",\"misconception\":\"Solve x^2+y^2=29 instead of the stated equation containing xy.\"},{\"option_id\":\"d\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Negate the y coordinate of the conjugate pair, retaining squared sum 22 but changing xy from 7 to -7; total becomes 15.\",\"misconception\":\"Assume changing one coordinate's sign preserves an expression because its squared terms are unchanged.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q39\",\"source_numbers\":[\"115\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Recognize two distinct solutions while respecting both radicand domains and the principal-root difference.\",\"source_solution\":\"x,y>=0; for t=sqrt(y)>=0, all solutions are ((t+1)^2,t^2). Taking t=0,1 gives (1,0),(4,1).\",\"unit_reason\":\"Selection of complete candidate pairs is one equation-level task, not separate values or blank clicks. It supplies two distinct recognized solutions without claiming construction.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Does not demonstrate generating the requested arbitrary two solutions independently.\",\"General solution production and original written solution generation remain deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\",\"c\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Both radicands are nonnegative and sqrt(1)-sqrt(0)=1.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Square the difference incorrectly as x-y=1; choose y=1 and x=2. Actually sqrt(2)-1≠1.\",\"misconception\":\"Omit the cross term when squaring a difference of square roots.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"sqrt(4)-sqrt(1)=2-1=1 with nonnegative radicands.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"d\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use the false squared condition x-y=1, set x=0 and y=-1, and fail to check the original radicands.\",\"misconception\":\"Combine an invalid squaring rule with omission of the y>=0 domain restriction.\"}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q40\",\"source_numbers\":[\"115\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Recognize two full solution pairs while enforcing y>=0 and x+y>=0.\",\"source_solution\":\"For t=sqrt(y)>=0, sqrt(x+y)=t+1 gives x=2t+1,y=t^2. Taking t=2,3 gives (5,4),(7,9); both radicands must be nonnegative.\",\"unit_reason\":\"Whole candidate pairs preserve the coupled radicands and provide two distinct recognized examples without atomizing the equation. Parameters 2,3 avoid repeating a correct candidate from the other radical task.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Selecting supplied pairs is not generating the source's arbitrary two solutions.\",\"Independent written solution generation remains deferred.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"b\",\"d\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Square the difference incorrectly to (x+y)-y=1, infer x=1, and choose y=4. Actual difference sqrt(5)-2≠1.\",\"misconception\":\"Treat the square of a difference as the difference of squares.\"},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"y=4 and x+y=9 are nonnegative; sqrt(9)-sqrt(4)=3-2=1.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Use the false squared condition x=1 and allow y=-1 because x+y=0 is nonnegative, forgetting the separate sqrt(y).\",\"misconception\":\"Check only the combined radicand while omitting y>=0, after invalid squaring.\"},{\"option_id\":\"d\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"y=9 and x+y=16 are in domain; sqrt(16)-sqrt(9)=4-3=1.\",\"misconception\":null}]}}\n{\"id\":\"q41\",\"source_numbers\":[\"116\"],\"intent\":{\"task\":\"Compare all four equations by sign and distinguish open quadrants from axis points without graphing.\",\"source_solution\":\"x^2+4>0 gives I,II and (0,4). 9-x^2 is positive for |x|<3 and negative outside, so all four quadrants, axis points (±3,0),(0,9). xy=6 requires matching signs, I,III; xy=-12 requires opposite signs, II,IV; neither meets an axis.\",\"unit_reason\":\"One complete sign/axis table retains the shared comparison and every boundary case rather than turning table cells into clicks.\",\"concept_blocks\":[1],\"limitations\":[\"Choosing a completed table does not demonstrate independently writing the sign analysis for each equation.\"],\"figure_observations\":[]},\"key\":{\"correct\":[\"a\"],\"rationales\":[{\"option_id\":\"a\",\"truth\":\"correct\",\"derivation\":\"Apply the stated sign intervals and product-sign rules; at x=0 the ordinates are 4,9, and the second equation alone has x-axis roots ±3.\",\"misconception\":null},{\"option_id\":\"b\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"Replace y=9-x^2 by y=sqrt(9-x^2), giving only I,II, x intercepts ±3 and y intercept 3; leave the other equations unchanged.\",\"misconception\":\"Insert an unstated square root, restricting domain and sign and changing the y-axis value.\"},{\"option_id\":\"c\",\"truth\":\"incorrect\",\"derivation\":\"From xy=6 conclude y>0 for both signs of x, and from xy=-12 conclude y<0 for both signs of x; this produces I,II and III,IV respectively.\",\"misconception\":\"Ignore the sign of the divisor x when deducing the sign of y=k/x.\"}]}}\n",
  "stderr": ""
}
